Hermes automation
  → list_extensions()
  → connect_extension({ nickname: 'research' }) ← { sessionBindingRef: 'sbr_research' }
  → connect_extension({ nickname: 'ads-sales' }) ← { sessionBindingRef: 'sbr_sales' }
  → browser_attach({ sessionBindingRef: 'sbr_research', tabId }) ← { debuggerSessionRef }
  → browser_click | browser_type | browser_wait | browser_snapshot | browser_screenshot
     or cdp_command({ sessionBindingRef, debuggerSessionRef, method, params })
  ← correlated result plus filtered events
  → detach and disconnect each profile independently

There is no implicit current browser. Every browser command identifies exactly one active profile session.

ConnectRequest { nickname, requestedTtlMs?, idempotencyKey? }
ConnectReply { sessionBindingRef, nickname, expiresAt, capabilities }
BrowserRequest { sessionBindingRef, requestId, operation, parameters, deadlineMs, idempotencyKey }
authenticate → RequestContext { principal, scopes, sessionBindingRef?, requestId, deadline }
The broker-issued sessionBindingRef explicitly represents the temporary relationship, so an MCP transport is never treated as the Hermes conversation identity.

BrokerCore.connect({ principal, nickname, ttl, idempotencyKey? }) → { sessionBindingRef, expiresAt, capabilities }
BrokerCore.dispatch({ principal, sessionBindingRef, requestId, deadline }, command) → { commandId, state, result? }
BrokerCore.disconnect({ principal, sessionBindingRef }) → { released: true }
MCP adapter + BrokerCore + SQLite + connection gateway remain one process; the broker is the sole source of session and routing truth.

TargetSnapshot { alias, privateTargetId, connectivity, health, occupancy, capabilities, lastSeen, errorWindow, status, statusVersion }
TargetStatus = facts only. It never contains deliver, queue, wait, or reject.
Only the broker reads privateTargetId or connection identifiers.

TargetStatus != RoutingDecision
RoutingDecision { disposition, reasonCode, evaluatedStatusVersion, retryAfter? }
A decision is valid only for one request evaluated against one status version.

browser_session_bindings { session_binding_id, session_binding_ref, principal_id, target_id, state, acquired_at, expires_at, released_at? }
UNIQUE active target_id; UNIQUE session_binding_ref
Connect: { nickname, requestedTtlMs?, idempotencyKey? }
Dispatch: { sessionBindingRef, requestId, operation, parameters, deadlineMs, idempotencyKey }
Broker validates the active lease, resolves target_id internally, and routes to the target's live socket.
